1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,950
This video shows you how to find out why a record never reached your SIEM.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,710
Your SIEM holds nothing for carol's DNS lookups or for bob's web traffic.

3
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:15,230
Both are in the records you captured from firewall.

4
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:16,632
Start with carol.

5
00:00:16,632 --> 00:00:18,699
Search for the name carol.

6
00:00:18,699 --> 00:00:24,706
A DNS lookup to port 53 shows an empty row: the pipeline dropped it.

7
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:29,569
The drop step, drop where dst_port == 53, dropped them.

8
00:00:29,569 --> 00:00:32,830
It shows how many records it dropped.

9
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:37,606
Keep only the dropped rows: every one is a lookup to port 53.

10
00:00:37,606 --> 00:00:41,925
A dropped record is not sent to any destination.

11
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:49,530
On Map, the pipeline's Steps tab counts what each step dropped in the running pipeline.

12
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:56,087
Now bob's web traffic.

13
00:00:56,087 --> 00:00:58,366
It did come out of the pipeline.

14
00:00:58,366 --> 00:01:02,100
Route a sample shows where each record goes.

15
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:07,290
Search for bob.

16
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:12,940
The row that allowed a connection to port 443 is bob's web traffic.

17
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:15,112
It went to archive only.

18
00:01:15,112 --> 00:01:21,310
siem takes a record only when risk is not low, and this record's risk is low.

19
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:24,620
Every record the pipeline kept went to archive.

20
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:28,757
Only those whose risk is not low also went to siem.

21
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,082
Check on learning.

22
00:01:32,282 --> 00:01:36,440
A record came out of the pipeline, but isn't in your SIEM.

23
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,259
What kept it out?

24
00:01:42,659 --> 00:01:46,099
The route's delivery condition for that destination.

25
00:01:46,099 --> 00:01:49,610
Route a sample shows where each record goes.

26
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:55,377
You can now read a pipeline from end to end: where a record came from,

27
00:01:55,377 --> 00:01:59,384
what changed it, and why it did or didn't arrive.
